export default [
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company needs persistent block storage for the root volume of a single Amazon EC2 instance. The data must survive instance stops. Which service should be used?","o":["Amazon EFS","Amazon EBS","Amazon S3","EC2 instance store"],"a":[1],"e":"EBS is persistent block storage attached to a single instance, so it suits a root volume that must survive stops. EFS is a shared file system for many instances, and instance store data is lost when the instance stops."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"Several Linux-based Amazon EC2 instances in different Availability Zones must read and write to the same file system at the same time using NFS. Which service meets this requirement?","o":["Amazon EBS","Amazon S3 Glacier","Amazon EFS","Amazon FSx for Windows File Server"],"a":[2],"e":"EFS is a managed NFS file system that many Linux instances across AZs can mount at the same time. FSx for Windows File Server uses SMB for Windows workloads, and an EBS volume is attached to one instance in one AZ."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company is moving Windows-based applications that use SMB file shares integrated with Microsoft Active Directory to AWS. Which service provides a fully managed native Windows file system?","o":["Amazon FSx for Windows File Server","Amazon EFS","Amazon FSx for Lustre","AWS Storage Gateway Volume Gateway"],"a":[0],"e":"FSx for Windows File Server provides native Windows SMB file shares integrated with Active Directory. EFS is NFS for Linux, and FSx for Lustre is a parallel file system for HPC."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A research team runs high performance computing (HPC) workloads that need a very fast parallel file system that can link directly to datasets in Amazon S3. Which service should they use?","o":["Amazon EFS","Amazon FSx for Lustre","Amazon EBS Provisioned IOPS","Amazon FSx for Windows File Server"],"a":[1],"e":"FSx for Lustre is a high-performance parallel file system for HPC/ML that links directly to S3 buckets. EFS is a general-purpose Linux file system, not optimized for large-scale HPC."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"An application needs very high I/O temporary storage for buffers and caches on an Amazon EC2 instance. The data can be lost if the instance is stopped. Which is the MOST cost-effective option?","o":["Amazon EBS Provisioned IOPS SSD","Amazon EFS","Amazon S3 Standard","EC2 instance store"],"a":[3],"e":"Instance store is physical disk on the host: very fast and included in the instance price, but its data is lost when the instance stops or terminates. EBS is persistent and billed separately."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company stores backups that are accessed rarely, but when needed they must be retrieved within milliseconds. The data must be stored across multiple Availability Zones. Which S3 storage class is MOST cost-effective?","o":["S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access","S3 Glacier Deep Archive","S3 Standard-Infrequent Access","S3 Standard"],"a":[2],"e":"S3 Standard-IA is for rarely accessed data that still needs millisecond access, stored across multiple AZs. One Zone-IA uses a single AZ, and Glacier Deep Archive takes hours to retrieve."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company has data in Amazon S3 with unknown and changing access patterns. It wants to optimize storage costs automatically without operational overhead or retrieval fees. Which storage class should it use?","o":["S3 Intelligent-Tiering","S3 Standard-Infrequent Access","S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval","S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access"],"a":[0],"e":"S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves objects between tiers based on access patterns, with no retrieval fees. Standard-IA suits data already known to be infrequently accessed and charges retrieval fees."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company must keep compliance records for 10 years. The records are almost never accessed, and a retrieval time of up to 12 hours is acceptable. Which S3 storage class has the LOWEST storage cost?","o":["S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval","S3 Standard-Infrequent Access","S3 Intelligent-Tiering","S3 Glacier Deep Archive"],"a":[3],"e":"S3 Glacier Deep Archive is the cheapest storage class for long-term archives, with retrieval within about 12 hours. Glacier Instant Retrieval costs more because it returns data in milliseconds."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A hospital archives medical images that are accessed about once per quarter. When a doctor requests an image, it must be available in milliseconds. Which S3 storage class is MOST cost-effective?","o":["S3 Glacier Deep Archive","S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval","S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval","S3 Standard"],"a":[1],"e":"S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval suits rarely accessed archive data (e.g. quarterly) that must still be retrievable in milliseconds. Glacier Flexible Retrieval and Deep Archive take minutes to hours."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company stores thumbnail images that can easily be regenerated from the originals. The thumbnails are accessed infrequently. Which S3 storage class is MOST cost-effective?","o":["S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access","S3 Standard","S3 Standard-Infrequent Access","S3 Intelligent-Tiering"],"a":[0],"e":"S3 One Zone-IA is cheaper because it stores data in a single AZ, which suits infrequently accessed data that can be regenerated. Standard-IA stores data across multiple AZs, so it is more resilient but costs more."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company wants its on-premises applications to use cloud storage through standard file protocols while keeping frequently used data cached locally. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS DataSync","AWS Transfer Family","AWS Storage Gateway","Amazon EFS"],"a":[2],"e":"Storage Gateway is a hybrid service that connects on-premises applications to AWS storage through standard file protocols with a local cache. DataSync moves or syncs data online, not ongoing cached access, and Transfer Family provides managed SFTP/FTPS/FTP endpoints."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company wants to centrally manage and automate backups across Amazon EBS, Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon EFS using backup plans. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon S3 Lifecycle","AWS Backup","AWS Storage Gateway","Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Backup centrally manages backups for many AWS services using backup plans. S3 Lifecycle only transitions or expires S3 objects, and Data Lifecycle Manager only automates EBS snapshots and EBS-backed AMIs."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company wants objects in an S3 bucket to move automatically to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval 90 days after creation and be deleted after 5 years. Which feature should it use?","o":["S3 Cross-Region Replication","S3 Versioning","S3 Transfer Acceleration","S3 Lifecycle configuration"],"a":[3],"e":"An S3 Lifecycle configuration transitions objects between storage classes and expires them based on object age. Replication copies objects to another bucket; it does not change storage classes."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"Users around the world upload large files to a single Amazon S3 bucket and experience slow uploads. Which feature improves upload speed to the bucket over long distances?","o":["S3 Transfer Acceleration","AWS Direct Connect","S3 Intelligent-Tiering","Amazon Route 53"],"a":[0],"e":"S3 Transfer Acceleration speeds up uploads to S3 by routing them through AWS edge locations. Direct Connect is a private link from a data center, not for users spread around the world."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company wants a point-in-time backup of an Amazon EBS volume that is stored durably and can be used to create new volumes in another Availability Zone. What should it use?","o":["EC2 instance store","Amazon EFS replication","Amazon EBS snapshot","S3 Lifecycle policy"],"a":[2],"e":"An EBS snapshot is a point-in-time backup of an EBS volume, stored in S3, that can create new volumes in another AZ. Instance store is not persistent and cannot be snapshotted."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"Which Amazon S3 features help protect objects from accidental deletion? (Choose TWO.)","o":["S3 Versioning","S3 Transfer Acceleration","MFA Delete","S3 Intelligent-Tiering","Requester Pays"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Versioning keeps previous object versions so they can be restored, and MFA Delete requires MFA to delete object versions. Transfer Acceleration only speeds up uploads, and Intelligent-Tiering optimizes cost."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"Which storage options can be accessed by multiple Amazon EC2 instances concurrently as a shared file system? (Choose TWO.)","o":["EC2 instance store","Amazon EFS","Amazon EBS gp3 volume","Amazon FSx for Windows File Server","S3 Glacier Deep Archive"],"a":[1,3],"e":"EFS (NFS) and FSx for Windows File Server (SMB) are shared file systems that many instances can access at once. Instance store and EBS gp3 volumes are tied to a single instance."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A startup wants to host a static website (HTML, CSS, images) with no servers to manage and high durability. Which service is the simplest choice?","o":["Amazon EC2 with Apache","Amazon EBS","Amazon S3","Amazon EFS"],"a":[2],"e":"S3 can host a static website directly with no servers to manage. EC2 requires managing servers, and EBS/EFS are storage that must be attached to compute."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A company needs a fully managed relational database that is MySQL- and PostgreSQL-compatible, offers higher performance than standard engines, and automatically replicates storage six ways across three Availability Zones. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon RDS for MySQL","Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon Redshift","Amazon Aurora"],"a":[3],"e":"Aurora is AWS's MySQL- and PostgreSQL-compatible relational database with higher performance and six copies of data across three AZs. RDS for MySQL is also a managed relational database but runs the standard engine."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A mobile gaming company needs a serverless NoSQL key-value database that delivers single-digit millisecond performance at any scale. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon Aurora Serverless","Amazon ElastiCache","Amazon DocumentDB"],"a":[0],"e":"DynamoDB is a serverless NoSQL key-value database with single-digit millisecond latency at any scale. Aurora Serverless is still relational, and ElastiCache is an in-memory cache, not a primary serverless key-value database."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A company wants to run complex analytical SQL queries across petabytes of structured historical data for business reporting. Which service is designed for this?","o":["Amazon RDS","Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon Redshift","Amazon Neptune"],"a":[2],"e":"Redshift is a petabyte-scale data warehouse for analytics (OLAP). RDS is for day-to-day transactional workloads (OLTP), not large-scale analytics."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"An application repeatedly reads the same data from an Amazon RDS database, causing high load. The company wants to add an in-memory caching layer to improve read performance. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Redshift","Amazon ElastiCache","Amazon Timestream for InfluxDB","Amazon RDS Multi-AZ"],"a":[1],"e":"ElastiCache (Redis OSS, Valkey, or Memcached) provides an in-memory cache that offloads repeated reads from the database. RDS Multi-AZ is for high availability, not faster reads."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A web application running on several Amazon EC2 instances needs a shared, managed in-memory store for user session data with sub-millisecond latency. Which service fits BEST?","o":["Amazon ElastiCache","Amazon Redshift","Amazon Neptune","S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval"],"a":[0],"e":"ElastiCache (Valkey, Redis OSS, or Memcached) is a managed in-memory data store commonly used for session state and caching with sub-millisecond latency. Redshift is a data warehouse, Neptune is a graph database, and Glacier is archive storage. (Amazon MemoryDB, a durable in-memory database, is not on the CLF-C02 in-scope service list.)"},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A social media company needs to store and query highly connected data, such as friends-of-friends relationships and recommendation paths. Which database service should it use?","o":["Amazon DocumentDB","Amazon Redshift","Amazon Timestream for InfluxDB","Amazon Neptune"],"a":[3],"e":"Neptune is a graph database for highly connected data such as social relationships, fraud graphs, and knowledge graphs. DocumentDB stores MongoDB-style JSON documents."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A company runs an application that uses MongoDB and wants to move it to a fully managed AWS database with minimal code changes. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon DocumentDB","Amazon Keyspaces","Amazon Neptune"],"a":[1],"e":"DocumentDB is a MongoDB-compatible document database, so the app needs minimal changes. Keyspaces is Apache Cassandra-compatible, and DynamoDB would require code changes."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A company runs a self-managed Apache Cassandra cluster for a high-traffic NoSQL key-value workload and is tired of patching and scaling nodes. It is willing to update its code and wants a serverless AWS NoSQL database with single-digit millisecond performance and no nodes to manage. Which service should it choose?","o":["Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon DocumentDB","Amazon Aurora","Amazon Neptune"],"a":[0],"e":"DynamoDB is AWS's serverless key-value and document NoSQL database with single-digit millisecond performance at any scale. DocumentDB is MongoDB-compatible, Aurora is relational, and Neptune is a graph database. (Amazon Keyspaces, the Cassandra-compatible service, is not on the CLF-C02 in-scope service list.)"},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A bank wants to detect fraud rings by quickly finding accounts that share phone numbers, devices, or addresses across many hops of relationships. Which purpose-built database is designed for this?","o":["Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL","Amazon Redshift","Amazon Neptune","Amazon ElastiCache"],"a":[2],"e":"Neptune is a managed graph database built to store and traverse highly connected relationships, which suits fraud detection, knowledge graphs, and recommendations. A relational database needs many expensive joins for multi-hop queries, Redshift is for analytics, and ElastiCache is an in-memory cache."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A company uses Amazon DynamoDB and needs microsecond read latency for a read-heavy workload with minimal application changes. Which service should it add?","o":["Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached","Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","Amazon Aurora Replicas","Amazon CloudFront"],"a":[1],"e":"DAX is an in-memory cache built for DynamoDB that delivers microsecond latency with minimal application changes. ElastiCache is also a cache, but the application must implement the caching logic itself."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A company needs its Amazon RDS database to automatically fail over to a standby in another Availability Zone if the primary fails. Which feature should it enable?","o":["Read replicas","Automated backups","RDS Multi-AZ deployment","Amazon RDS Proxy"],"a":[2],"e":"Multi-AZ keeps a standby in another AZ and fails over to it automatically for high availability. Read replicas scale read traffic; they are not the automatic failover mechanism."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A company's reporting queries are slowing down its production Amazon RDS database. It wants to offload read-only reporting traffic to separate copies of the database. Which feature should it use?","o":["RDS Multi-AZ standby","RDS read replicas","RDS automated snapshots","Amazon ElastiCache Global Datastore"],"a":[1],"e":"Read replicas serve read traffic, offloading reporting queries from the primary database. The standby in a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment does not serve reads; it exists only for failover."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A company must run a database that requires full control over the operating system and custom software installed on the database server. Which option should it choose?","o":["Amazon RDS","Amazon Aurora","Amazon DynamoDB","A self-managed database on Amazon EC2"],"a":[3],"e":"If you need full control of the OS, run a self-managed database on EC2, where the customer handles OS and database patching. Standard RDS and Aurora are managed services where AWS manages the OS, so you have no OS access (only the specialized RDS Custom option allows limited OS customization)."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A global e-commerce company needs a NoSQL database that replicates tables across multiple AWS Regions with active-active (multi-Region, multi-active) writes. Which feature should it use?","o":["Amazon DynamoDB global tables","Amazon Aurora Global Database","Amazon RDS cross-Region read replicas","Amazon Redshift data sharing"],"a":[0],"e":"DynamoDB global tables are multi-Region, multi-active NoSQL tables that accept writes in every Region. Aurora Global Database is relational, and its secondary Regions are mainly for reads and DR."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"Which AWS services are NoSQL databases? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon Aurora","Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon Redshift","Amazon DocumentDB","Amazon RDS for MySQL"],"a":[1,3],"e":"DynamoDB (key-value) and DocumentDB (document) are NoSQL databases. Aurora and RDS for MySQL are relational, and Redshift is a data warehouse."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"Which use cases are a good fit for Amazon ElastiCache? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Caching frequently read database query results","Long-term archival of compliance records","Storing user session state for a web application","Running a petabyte-scale data warehouse","Storing virtual machine boot volumes"],"a":[0,2],"e":"ElastiCache keeps data in memory for sub-millisecond reads, so it is used for caching query results and storing session state. Archival belongs in S3 Glacier, data warehousing in Redshift, and boot volumes in EBS."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A company has an online transaction processing (OLTP) application that requires a relational database with joins and ACID transactions. The company does not want to manage database patching or backups. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Redshift","Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon RDS","Amazon EMR"],"a":[2],"e":"RDS is a managed relational database for OLTP with joins and ACID transactions; AWS handles patching and backups. Redshift is for analytics (OLAP), not an application's transactional workload."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A company wants to deliver videos and static content to global users with low latency by caching content at edge locations. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Global Accelerator","Amazon Route 53","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Direct Connect"],"a":[2],"e":"CloudFront is a CDN that caches content at edge locations close to users. Global Accelerator does not cache; it routes traffic over the AWS global network using static anycast IPs."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A multiplayer gaming company uses UDP traffic and needs two static anycast IP addresses that route players over the AWS global network to the nearest healthy Regional endpoint. Content caching is NOT needed. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon CloudFront","AWS Global Accelerator","Amazon Route 53 latency routing","Elastic Load Balancing"],"a":[1],"e":"Global Accelerator provides two static anycast IPs and accelerates TCP/UDP traffic over the AWS network without caching. CloudFront focuses on caching HTTP(S) content."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A company needs to register a domain name and route users to the AWS Region that provides the lowest latency. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon CloudFront","AWS Global Accelerator","Elastic Load Balancing","Amazon Route 53"],"a":[3],"e":"Route 53 is a DNS service that can register domains and offers latency-based routing. Global Accelerator uses static IPs and is not a DNS or domain registration service."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A financial company needs a dedicated, private network connection from its data center to AWS with consistent bandwidth that does not traverse the public internet. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Direct Connect","AWS Site-to-Site VPN","Amazon CloudFront","VPC peering"],"a":[0],"e":"Direct Connect is a dedicated private physical connection to AWS with consistent performance. Site-to-Site VPN is encrypted but still travels over the public internet, so performance can vary."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A company needs to quickly set up an encrypted connection between its on-premises network and a VPC over the internet. It cannot wait weeks for a physical circuit. Which option should it use?","o":["AWS Direct Connect","AWS Site-to-Site VPN","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Transit Gateway peering"],"a":[1],"e":"Site-to-Site VPN can be set up quickly and encrypts traffic with IPsec over the internet. Direct Connect requires installing a physical circuit, which can take weeks."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A company has more than 100 VPCs and several on-premises networks. It wants a central hub to interconnect all of them and simplify routing. Which service should it use?","o":["VPC peering","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Transit Gateway","Internet gateway"],"a":[2],"e":"Transit Gateway acts as a central hub (hub-and-spoke) for many VPCs and on-premises networks. VPC peering is one-to-one and non-transitive, so it becomes unwieldy with many VPCs."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A company needs to connect two VPCs so that instances in both can communicate using private IP addresses. Only these two VPCs are involved. What is the SIMPLEST solution?","o":["VPC peering","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Direct Connect","NAT gateway"],"a":[0],"e":"VPC peering is the simplest way to privately connect two VPCs. Transit Gateway is better suited to connecting many VPCs."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A SaaS provider wants to expose a service in its VPC privately to customer VPCs without VPC peering and without exposing traffic to the internet. Which service should it use?","o":["Internet gateway","AWS PrivateLink","VPC peering","Amazon CloudFront"],"a":[1],"e":"PrivateLink exposes a single service privately through interface endpoints, without peering or the internet. VPC peering opens full network connectivity between VPCs, not just one service."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"Amazon EC2 instances in a private subnet need to download software updates from the internet, but must not accept inbound connections initiated from the internet. What should be used?","o":["Internet gateway attached to the private subnet","Elastic IP address on each instance","VPC peering","NAT gateway"],"a":[3],"e":"A NAT gateway (placed in a public subnet) lets instances in a private subnet reach the internet outbound while blocking inbound connections. An internet gateway gives public subnets two-way internet access."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"Which VPC component allows resources in a public subnet to communicate directly with the internet?","o":["NAT gateway","Internet gateway","Virtual private gateway","Gateway VPC endpoint"],"a":[1],"e":"An internet gateway connects a VPC's public subnets to the internet. A virtual private gateway is the AWS side of a VPN or Direct Connect connection, not an internet path."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"EC2 instances in a private subnet must access Amazon S3 without using the internet, a NAT gateway, or a VPN. What should be configured?","o":["A gateway VPC endpoint for Amazon S3","An internet gateway","AWS Direct Connect","S3 Transfer Acceleration"],"a":[0],"e":"A gateway VPC endpoint gives private access to S3 (and DynamoDB) from a VPC without the internet. Transfer Acceleration speeds transfers through public edge locations instead."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"Which VPC component determines where network traffic from a subnet is directed?","o":["Security group","Network ACL","Route table","Elastic IP address"],"a":[2],"e":"A route table contains routes that decide where traffic from a subnet is sent. Security groups and network ACLs are firewalls that allow or deny traffic, not direct it."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A company needs a firewall at the subnet level that is stateless and supports explicit DENY rules. Which VPC feature should it use?","o":["Security group","Network ACL","AWS WAF","Route table"],"a":[1],"e":"A network ACL works at the subnet level, is stateless, and supports both allow and deny rules. A security group works at the instance level, is stateful, and has allow rules only."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A web application needs a load balancer that routes HTTP requests to different target groups based on the URL path (for example, /api and /images). Which load balancer should it use?","o":["Network Load Balancer","Gateway Load Balancer","Application Load Balancer","Amazon Route 53"],"a":[2],"e":"An Application Load Balancer works at Layer 7 and can route by HTTP path or host. A Network Load Balancer works at Layer 4 (TCP/UDP) and does not inspect HTTP content."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A trading application needs a load balancer that handles millions of requests per second with ultra-low latency for TCP and UDP traffic and supports a static IP per Availability Zone. Which should it use?","o":["Network Load Balancer","Application Load Balancer","Gateway Load Balancer","Amazon CloudFront"],"a":[0],"e":"A Network Load Balancer (Layer 4) is built for ultra-low-latency TCP/UDP traffic at millions of requests per second and supports a static IP per AZ. An ALB handles HTTP/HTTPS with content-based routing."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A company wants to deploy, scale, and manage a fleet of third-party virtual firewall appliances to inspect traffic. Which load balancer type is designed for this?","o":["Application Load Balancer","Network Load Balancer","Classic Load Balancer","Gateway Load Balancer"],"a":[3],"e":"A Gateway Load Balancer is purpose-built to deploy and scale third-party virtual appliances such as firewalls and IDS/IPS. ALB and NLB distribute traffic to applications."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A company needs a static public IPv4 address that can be quickly remapped to a different Amazon EC2 instance if the original instance fails. What should it use?","o":["Private IP address","Elastic IP address","Customer-managed prefix list","Auto-assigned public IP"],"a":[1],"e":"An Elastic IP is a static public IPv4 address that you can remap to another instance. An auto-assigned public IP can change when the instance is stopped and started, a private IP is not reachable from the internet, and a prefix list is just a set of CIDR blocks used in route tables and security groups."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"Which of the following are Amazon Route 53 routing policies? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Path-based routing","Failover routing","Least outstanding requests","Geolocation routing","Sticky sessions"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Failover and geolocation are Route 53 DNS routing policies. Path-based routing, least outstanding requests, and sticky sessions are Application Load Balancer features."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"Which options can connect an on-premises data center to an Amazon VPC? (Choose TWO.)","o":["AWS Direct Connect","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Site-to-Site VPN","VPC peering","Gateway VPC endpoint"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Direct Connect (dedicated private link) and Site-to-Site VPN (encrypted over the internet) connect on-premises networks to a VPC. VPC peering only connects VPCs to other VPCs."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"A company runs web servers and small databases with a balanced need for compute, memory, and networking. Which EC2 instance family category is MOST appropriate?","o":["Compute optimized","Memory optimized","General purpose","Storage optimized"],"a":[2],"e":"General purpose instances (e.g. M and T families) balance CPU, memory, and networking, which suits web servers. Compute optimized is for CPU-heavy workloads."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"A company runs batch processing, scientific modeling, and dedicated gaming servers that require high-performance processors. Which EC2 instance category should it choose?","o":["Compute optimized","Memory optimized","Storage optimized","General purpose"],"a":[0],"e":"Compute optimized instances (C family) suit CPU-heavy workloads such as batch processing, gaming servers, and scientific modeling. Memory optimized is for large in-memory datasets."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"A company needs to process very large datasets in memory for a real-time analytics application. Which EC2 instance category is MOST suitable?","o":["Compute optimized","Accelerated computing","Storage optimized","Memory optimized"],"a":[3],"e":"Memory optimized instances (e.g. R and X families) are designed to process large datasets in memory. Compute optimized focuses on CPU, not RAM capacity."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"A workload requires high, sequential read and write access to very large datasets on local storage, such as a distributed file system or data warehouse. Which EC2 instance category should be used?","o":["General purpose","Storage optimized","Memory optimized","Compute optimized"],"a":[1],"e":"Storage optimized instances (e.g. I and D families) deliver high throughput and IOPS to local storage for large datasets. Memory optimized focuses on RAM, not local disk throughput."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"A data science team needs to train deep learning models using GPUs. Which EC2 instance category should it use?","o":["Accelerated computing","Memory optimized","General purpose","Storage optimized"],"a":[0],"e":"Accelerated computing instances (e.g. P and G families) use GPUs or hardware accelerators for ML and graphics. Memory optimized only adds RAM, with no GPU."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"A company wants to run code in response to events, such as an object being uploaded to Amazon S3, without provisioning or managing servers. Each execution finishes in a few seconds. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon EC2","AWS Elastic Beanstalk","AWS Lambda","Amazon Lightsail"],"a":[2],"e":"Lambda runs code serverlessly in response to events (up to 15 minutes per invocation). Elastic Beanstalk still runs EC2 instances behind the scenes for always-on applications."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"A company wants to automatically add Amazon EC2 instances when demand increases and remove them when demand decreases. Which service should it use?","o":["Elastic Load Balancing","Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","AWS Auto Recovery","Amazon CloudWatch"],"a":[1],"e":"EC2 Auto Scaling adds or removes instances to match demand. ELB only distributes traffic across existing instances, and CloudWatch monitors metrics (it can trigger scaling but does not launch instances)."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"A company wants to launch many identical EC2 instances that already include its operating system configuration and preinstalled software. What should it use?","o":["Amazon EBS snapshot only","AWS CloudFormation StackSet","Instance user data only","Amazon Machine Image (AMI)"],"a":[3],"e":"An AMI is a template containing the OS and software used to launch identical instances. An EBS snapshot is only a volume backup, not a complete launch template."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"A small business with limited cloud experience wants to launch a simple WordPress website on a virtual private server with a low, predictable monthly price. Which service is the BEST fit?","o":["Amazon Lightsail","Amazon EC2 with Auto Scaling","AWS Elastic Beanstalk","Amazon EKS"],"a":[0],"e":"Lightsail offers simple virtual private servers with predictable monthly pricing, ideal for beginners and simple websites. EC2 is more flexible but requires much more configuration."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"Developers want to upload their Java web application code and have AWS automatically handle capacity provisioning, load balancing, auto scaling, and health monitoring, while still allowing access to the underlying EC2 instances. Which service should they use?","o":["AWS Lambda","AWS CloudFormation","AWS Elastic Beanstalk","Amazon Lightsail"],"a":[2],"e":"Elastic Beanstalk (PaaS) takes uploaded code and handles provisioning, load balancing, scaling, and health monitoring on EC2. CloudFormation only creates resources from templates you design yourself."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"A company needs to run hundreds of thousands of batch computing jobs, with AWS dynamically provisioning the optimal quantity and type of compute resources. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Step Functions","AWS Batch","Amazon EMR","Amazon SQS"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Batch schedules and runs batch jobs and provisions the optimal compute automatically. Step Functions orchestrates workflows; it does not manage compute capacity for batch jobs."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"Which AWS services let you run application code WITHOUT provisioning or managing servers? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon EC2","AWS Lambda","Amazon Lightsail","AWS Fargate","AWS Outposts"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Lambda (functions) and Fargate (containers) are serverless compute. EC2 and Lightsail still require you to manage servers or instances."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"An EC2 instance in an Auto Scaling group fails its health check. What does Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling do?","o":["Sends the failed instance to AWS Support","Moves the instance to another Region","Terminates the unhealthy instance and launches a replacement","Converts the instance into a Spot Instance"],"a":[2],"e":"Auto Scaling automatically terminates an unhealthy instance and launches a replacement to maintain the desired capacity. It does not move instances to another Region."},
  {"d":3,"t":"containers","q":"A company wants a fully managed, AWS-native container orchestration service that is deeply integrated with AWS and does not require Kubernetes knowledge. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon EKS","Amazon ECS","Amazon ECR","AWS Batch"],"a":[1],"e":"ECS is AWS's own container orchestration service and requires no Kubernetes knowledge. EKS is managed Kubernetes, suited to teams already using Kubernetes."},
  {"d":3,"t":"containers","q":"A company already uses Kubernetes on premises and wants to run its Kubernetes workloads on AWS with a managed control plane. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon EKS","Amazon ECS","AWS Fargate","Amazon ECR"],"a":[0],"e":"EKS runs Kubernetes with an AWS-managed control plane. ECS also orchestrates containers, but it is not Kubernetes."},
  {"d":3,"t":"containers","q":"A team runs containers on Amazon ECS and wants to stop managing, patching, and scaling the underlying EC2 instances. Which option should it use?","o":["Amazon ECR","Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","AWS Fargate","Amazon Lightsail containers"],"a":[2],"e":"Fargate is serverless compute for containers (ECS and EKS), so there are no EC2 instances to manage. ECR is only a registry for storing container images."},
  {"d":3,"t":"containers","q":"A development team needs a fully managed private registry to store, share, and version Docker container images. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon ECS","Amazon S3","AWS CodeArtifact","Amazon ECR"],"a":[3],"e":"ECR (Elastic Container Registry) is a managed registry for container images. CodeArtifact is for software packages (npm, Maven, pip), not Docker images."},
  {"d":3,"t":"containers","q":"Which AWS services can run and orchestrate containers? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon ECR","Amazon ECS","Amazon Inspector","Amazon EKS","Amazon S3"],"a":[1,3],"e":"ECS and EKS are orchestration services that run containers. ECR only stores container images; it does not run them."},
  {"d":3,"t":"containers","q":"A company wants to run containers on Amazon EKS and keep full control over the worker node operating system and instance types. Which compute option should it use for the worker nodes?","o":["Amazon EC2 instances","AWS Fargate","AWS Lambda","Amazon ECR"],"a":[0],"e":"With EC2 worker nodes you keep full control over the OS and instance types. Fargate is serverless, so you cannot manage the host OS."},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"A company wants to decouple its order service from its payment service so that messages are stored until the payment service polls and processes them. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon SNS","Amazon SQS","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams"],"a":[1],"e":"SQS is a pull-based message queue: messages are stored until a consumer polls and processes them. SNS pushes messages to many subscribers (pub/sub) and does not store them for polling."},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"An application must send the same notification simultaneously to email subscribers, SMS recipients, and multiple Amazon SQS queues using a publish/subscribe model. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon SQS","Amazon MQ","Amazon SNS","AWS Step Functions"],"a":[2],"e":"SNS is a pub/sub service that pushes one message to many subscribers (email, SMS, SQS, Lambda). SQS is a queue that consumers poll."},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"A company wants to route events from AWS services and third-party SaaS applications to different targets based on rules that match event content. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon EventBridge","Amazon SQS","Amazon SNS","Amazon MQ"],"a":[0],"e":"EventBridge is a serverless event bus that receives events from AWS services, your own apps, and SaaS partners, and routes them by content-matching rules. SNS lacks SaaS event integrations and such rich rules."},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"A company is moving an on-premises application that uses a self-managed message queue to pass orders from its web tier to its processing tier. It wants a fully managed, serverless queue that scales automatically and stores messages until they are processed. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon SNS","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon SQS","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams"],"a":[2],"e":"Amazon SQS is a fully managed message queue that decouples tiers and holds messages until consumers process and delete them. SNS pushes messages to subscribers without queuing them for later, and EventBridge routes events by rules. (Amazon MQ, a managed ActiveMQ/RabbitMQ broker, is not on the CLF-C02 in-scope service list.)"},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"A company wants to coordinate several AWS Lambda functions into a visual workflow with sequential steps, branching, error handling, and automatic retries. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon SQS","AWS Step Functions","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon SNS"],"a":[1],"e":"Step Functions orchestrates steps into a visual state machine with branching, retries, and error handling. EventBridge only routes events; it does not control workflow order."},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"A banking application requires messages to be processed exactly once and in the exact order they are sent. Which option should be used?","o":["Amazon SQS standard queue","Amazon SNS standard topic","Amazon SQS FIFO queue","Amazon Data Firehose"],"a":[2],"e":"An SQS FIFO queue guarantees message ordering and exactly-once processing. An SQS standard queue offers higher throughput but only best-effort ordering and possible duplicates."},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"A company wants to create, publish, secure, and monitor RESTful APIs that invoke AWS Lambda functions at any scale. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon API Gateway","AWS AppSync","Elastic Load Balancing","Amazon CloudFront"],"a":[0],"e":"API Gateway creates and manages REST, HTTP, and WebSocket APIs as the front door to backends such as Lambda. AppSync is for GraphQL APIs."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A small mobile team wants to add user sign-in, a cloud data store, and APIs to its iOS, Android, and web apps using client libraries and a CLI, without managing backend infrastructure. Which service is designed for this?","o":["Amazon API Gateway","AWS Amplify","Amazon EventBridge","AWS Elastic Beanstalk"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Amplify provides libraries, a CLI, and hosting for full-stack web and mobile apps and connects them to managed backends for authentication (Amazon Cognito), data, and APIs. API Gateway only manages APIs, and Elastic Beanstalk deploys server-side web apps. (AWS AppSync, a managed GraphQL service, is not on the CLF-C02 in-scope service list.)"},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"A company needs to invoke an AWS Lambda function automatically every day at 2 AM on a schedule. Which service is the BEST choice?","o":["Amazon SQS","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon SNS"],"a":[2],"e":"EventBridge (scheduled rules or EventBridge Scheduler) can invoke targets such as Lambda on a cron schedule. SNS and SQS have no cron scheduling."},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"Which AWS services are primarily used to decouple components of a distributed application through messaging? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon CloudFront","Amazon SQS","Amazon Route 53","Amazon SNS","Amazon EBS"],"a":[1,3],"e":"SQS (queue) and SNS (pub/sub) are messaging services used for decoupling. CloudFront is a CDN and Route 53 is DNS."},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"An order event must be delivered to three different microservices, and each microservice must process it at its own pace with its own retry buffer. Which architecture is MOST appropriate?","o":["One Amazon SQS queue shared by all three services","Amazon SNS topic fanning out to three Amazon SQS queues","Amazon Data Firehose to Amazon S3","AWS Step Functions with a single Lambda function"],"a":[1],"e":"The SNS-to-SQS fan-out pattern gives each service its own copy of the message in its own queue. A single shared queue means each message is processed by only one consumer."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A company wants to run ad hoc SQL queries directly on log files stored in Amazon S3 without loading the data or managing any servers. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Redshift","Amazon EMR","Amazon Athena","AWS Glue"],"a":[2],"e":"Athena is a serverless SQL query engine that reads data directly in S3, billed per data scanned. Redshift is a data warehouse that usually requires loading data into a cluster."},
  {"d": 3, "t": "analytics", "q": "A company needs a serverless service to discover data with crawlers, maintain a central data catalog, and run extract, transform, and load (ETL) jobs. Which service should it use?", "o": ["AWS Glue", "Amazon Athena", "Amazon Quick", "Amazon Kinesis Data Streams"], "a": [0], "e": "Glue is a serverless ETL service with crawlers and a central Data Catalog. Athena only runs SQL queries and uses the Glue Data Catalog for metadata."},
  {"d": 3, "t": "analytics", "q": "A data engineering team wants to run big data frameworks such as Apache Spark, Hive, and Hadoop on a managed cluster for petabyte-scale processing. Which service should it use?", "o": ["AWS Glue DataBrew", "Amazon EMR", "Amazon Athena", "Amazon Quick"], "a": [1], "e": "EMR is a managed big data platform for running Spark, Hadoop, Hive, and similar frameworks. Athena is serverless SQL querying, not a big data cluster."},
  {"d": 3, "t": "analytics", "q": "Business users need interactive dashboards and visualizations built from data in Amazon Redshift and Amazon S3, including natural-language questions. Which service should they use?", "o": ["Amazon Athena", "Amazon OpenSearch Service", "AWS Glue", "Amazon Quick"], "a": [3], "e": "Amazon Quick (formerly Amazon QuickSight) provides business intelligence (BI) dashboards, visualizations, and natural-language questions. Athena only runs queries and has no BI dashboards."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A company needs to perform full-text search and real-time log analytics on application logs, with dashboards for visualization. Which service is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon Redshift","Amazon Athena","AWS Glue"],"a":[0],"e":"OpenSearch Service handles full-text search and log analytics, with OpenSearch Dashboards for visualization. Athena runs ad hoc SQL on data in S3, Redshift is a data warehouse, and Glue is an ETL service; none provides full-text search."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"Which AWS service is the most common storage foundation for a data lake, offering virtually unlimited, durable, low-cost object storage that analytics services such as Amazon Athena and Amazon EMR can query directly?","o":["Amazon EBS","Amazon S3","Amazon RDS","Amazon ElastiCache"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon S3 is the usual data lake store: it scales without limit, is designed for 11 nines of durability, and is queried in place by Athena, EMR, Redshift Spectrum, and AWS Glue. EBS volumes attach to single instances, RDS is a relational database, and ElastiCache is an in-memory cache. (AWS Lake Formation, which manages data lake permissions, is not on the CLF-C02 in-scope service list.)"},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A company wants to replace its self-managed streaming cluster with a fully managed AWS service for ingesting real-time data from thousands of sources, and it is willing to update its producers and consumers to use the new service's API. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon SQS","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon Athena","AWS Glue"],"a":[1],"e":"Kinesis Data Streams is AWS's managed service for real-time streaming ingestion with multiple consumers that can replay data. SQS is a message queue, and Athena and Glue work on stored data. (Amazon MSK, managed Apache Kafka, is not on the CLF-C02 in-scope service list.)"},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A company wants to capture clickstream data in real time and allow multiple custom consumer applications to process and replay the stream. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Data Firehose","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon SQS","Amazon SNS"],"a":[1],"e":"Kinesis Data Streams supports real-time streaming with multiple custom consumers and replay within the retention period. Data Firehose delivers data to destinations (S3, Redshift, OpenSearch) without custom consumers."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A company wants to load streaming data into Amazon S3 and Amazon Redshift with automatic scaling and no custom consumer code or stream management. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Data Firehose","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon MSK","AWS Glue crawlers"],"a":[0],"e":"Amazon Data Firehose (formerly Kinesis Data Firehose) is the easiest way to load streaming data into S3, Redshift, or OpenSearch with no consumer code. Kinesis Data Streams requires your own consumer applications."},
  {"d": 3, "t": "analytics", "q": "Which AWS services can run SQL queries directly against data stored in Amazon S3? (Choose TWO.)", "o": ["Amazon Quick", "Amazon Athena", "Amazon MQ", "Amazon Redshift Spectrum", "AWS Lake Formation"], "a": [1, 3], "e": "Athena and Redshift Spectrum can query data directly in S3 using SQL. Amazon Quick (formerly QuickSight) is for visualization, and Lake Formation manages the data lake and its permissions."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"Which AWS services are designed for ingesting real-time streaming data? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon Athena","Amazon Quick","Amazon Data Firehose","S3 Glacier Deep Archive"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Kinesis Data Streams and Amazon Data Firehose (formerly Kinesis Data Firehose) both ingest real-time streaming data. Athena runs ad hoc queries on stored data, Amazon Quick (formerly QuickSight) is for dashboards, and Glacier Deep Archive is archive storage."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"A data science team wants to build, train, and deploy its own custom machine learning models with managed notebooks and infrastructure. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Bedrock","Amazon Rekognition","Amazon SageMaker AI","Amazon Comprehend"],"a":[2],"e":"SageMaker AI is a platform for building, training, and deploying your own ML models. Bedrock provides ready-made foundation models through an API rather than training models from scratch."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"A company wants to start from a pre-trained foundation model, fine-tune it with its own data, and deploy it to a managed endpoint for a generative AI application. Which AWS service provides this?","o":["Amazon SageMaker AI","Amazon Polly","Amazon Lex","Amazon Rekognition"],"a":[0],"e":"SageMaker AI (through SageMaker JumpStart) offers pre-trained foundation models that you can fine-tune and deploy to managed endpoints. Polly, Lex, and Rekognition are pre-trained AI services for speech, chatbots, and images. (Amazon Bedrock is not on the CLF-C02 in-scope service list.)"},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"Employees want a generative AI assistant that can answer questions, summarize content, and generate content using the company's internal data and enterprise systems. Which service is designed for this?","o":["Amazon Lex","Amazon Q Business","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Polly"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon Q Business is a generative AI assistant for work that connects to company data and enterprise applications to answer questions, summarize, and create content. Lex is for building your own chatbots (intents and slots), not a ready-made workplace assistant. Note: AWS now points new customers to Amazon Quick for this, but the exam guide lists Amazon Q."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"Developers want a generative AI assistant in their IDE and command line that generates code from natural-language prompts, explains code, and helps write tests. Which AWS service should they use?","o":["Amazon SageMaker AI","Amazon Lex","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Q Developer"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon Q Developer is the generative AI coding assistant for IDEs and the command line: it generates, explains, and tests code. SageMaker AI is for building and training ML models, and Lex builds chatbots. Note: AWS has since moved new IDE users to Kiro, but the exam guide lists Amazon Q."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"A company needs to detect faces, objects, and inappropriate content in images and videos uploaded by users. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Textract","Amazon Rekognition","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Transcribe"],"a":[1],"e":"Rekognition analyzes images and video for faces, objects, and content moderation. Textract extracts text and data from documents; it does not recognize objects or faces."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"A company wants to analyze customer reviews that are already in text form to determine sentiment and extract key phrases and entities. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Textract","Amazon Translate","Amazon Rekognition"],"a":[0],"e":"Comprehend is an NLP service for sentiment, key phrases, and entities in text. Textract pulls text out of documents and scans; it does not analyze meaning."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"An insurance company wants to automatically extract printed text, handwriting, form fields, and tables from scanned claim documents. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Rekognition","Amazon Textract","Amazon Transcribe"],"a":[2],"e":"Textract extracts printed text, handwriting, forms (key-value pairs), and tables from scanned documents. Comprehend analyzes text that already exists; it cannot read scanned documents."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"A call center wants to automatically convert recorded customer phone calls into written transcripts. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Polly","Amazon Translate","Amazon Lex","Amazon Transcribe"],"a":[3],"e":"Transcribe converts speech to text. Polly does the reverse, converting text to speech."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"An e-learning company wants to convert written lesson text into lifelike spoken audio in multiple voices. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Polly","Amazon Lex","Amazon Comprehend"],"a":[1],"e":"Polly converts text into lifelike speech (text-to-speech) in many voices. Transcribe converts speech to text."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"A company wants to automatically translate its product descriptions from English into Spanish, Japanese, and Indonesian. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Translate","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Polly","Amazon Transcribe"],"a":[0],"e":"Translate is a neural machine translation service between languages. Comprehend can detect language and sentiment but does not translate."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"A company wants to build a conversational chatbot that understands user intent through voice and text for its customer support website. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Polly","Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Lex","Amazon Personalize"],"a":[2],"e":"Lex builds conversational chatbots (voice and text) that understand user intent. Polly only converts text to speech, Transcribe only converts speech to text, and Personalize generates recommendations."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"A company wants employees to ask natural-language questions and get answers grounded in its own documents stored in Amazon S3 and SharePoint, using a fully managed service that handles connecting, indexing, and retrieval. Which should it use?","o":["Amazon Athena","Amazon Q Business","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Textract"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon Q Business has built-in connectors for sources such as S3 and SharePoint, indexes the content, and answers questions grounded in it while respecting user permissions. Athena runs SQL on structured data, and Comprehend and Textract analyze or extract text but do not answer questions. (Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases and Amazon Kendra are not on the CLF-C02 in-scope service list.)"},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"An online retailer's data science team wants to build and train its own product-recommendation model on customer browsing data, then deploy it to a managed endpoint. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Rekognition","Amazon Lex","Amazon SageMaker AI"],"a":[3],"e":"SageMaker AI is the platform for building, training, and deploying custom ML models such as a recommendation model. Comprehend analyzes text, Rekognition analyzes images, and Lex builds chatbots. (Amazon Personalize, a ready-made recommendation service, is not on the CLF-C02 in-scope service list.)"},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"A company is building a voice application that converts customers' spoken questions into text and then replies with spoken audio. Which services should it use? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Textract","Amazon Polly","Amazon Translate","Amazon Comprehend"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Transcribe converts the customer's speech to text, and Polly turns the text reply into speech. Textract is for documents and Translate is for language translation."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A team needs a fully managed service that compiles source code, runs unit tests, and produces deployable artifacts without managing build servers. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS CodeDeploy","AWS CodePipeline","AWS CodeBuild","AWS CodeArtifact"],"a":[2],"e":"CodeBuild is a managed build service that compiles code, runs tests, and produces artifacts. CodeDeploy only deploys build output to EC2, on-premises servers, Lambda, or ECS."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"A developer wants to upload a new version of a web application and have AWS roll it out across the Amazon EC2 instances in batches, keeping the app available, without writing deployment scripts. Which service should they use?","o":["AWS Elastic Beanstalk","AWS CodeBuild","Amazon ECR","AWS CloudTrail"],"a":[0],"e":"Elastic Beanstalk deploys new application versions for you, with policies such as rolling deployments that keep the app serving traffic. CodeBuild only builds and tests code, ECR stores container images, and CloudTrail logs API calls. (AWS CodeDeploy is not on the CLF-C02 in-scope service list.)"},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A company wants to model and automate the full release process, including source, build, test, and deploy stages, every time code changes. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS CodeBuild","AWS CodeDeploy","AWS CodeArtifact","AWS CodePipeline"],"a":[3],"e":"CodePipeline orchestrates the whole CI/CD release process from source to deploy. CodeBuild and CodeDeploy are each just one stage within that pipeline."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A team's self-hosted build server is often overloaded when many developers push code at once. The team wants a fully managed build service that runs builds concurrently, scales automatically, and charges by build minute. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS CodePipeline","AWS CodeBuild","Amazon ECR","AWS X-Ray"],"a":[1],"e":"CodeBuild is a fully managed build service that compiles code and runs tests in on-demand containers, scaling automatically and billing per build minute. CodePipeline orchestrates release stages but does not build code itself."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"An administrator who is new to AWS wants to create and manage resources through a web-based graphical interface instead of writing commands or code. Which should they use?","o":["AWS CLI","AWS Management Console","AWS SDK for Python (Boto3)","AWS CloudFormation"],"a":[1],"e":"The AWS Management Console is the browser-based graphical interface for managing AWS services. The AWS CLI and SDKs are programmatic, and CloudFormation provisions resources from templates."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A frontend developer wants to quickly build, deploy, and host a full-stack web and mobile application with managed CI/CD hosting and backend features like authentication. Which service should they use?","o":["AWS Amplify","AWS Elastic Beanstalk","Amazon Lightsail","AWS AppSync"],"a":[0],"e":"Amplify is designed for frontend and mobile developers to quickly build and host full-stack apps with CI/CD and features like authentication. Elastic Beanstalk deploys server-side applications on EC2."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A developer wants to interact with AWS services programmatically from inside a Python application. Which option should they use?","o":["AWS Management Console","AWS CloudShell","AWS SDK for Python (Boto3)","AWS CloudFormation console"],"a":[2],"e":"An AWS SDK (e.g. Boto3 for Python) calls AWS APIs from within application code. CloudShell is an interactive shell for people, not a library inside an application."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company wants to provision and manage AWS resources by using declarative JSON or YAML templates so that environments can be recreated consistently. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Elastic Beanstalk","AWS Systems Manager","AWS Service Catalog","AWS CloudFormation"],"a":[3],"e":"CloudFormation is infrastructure as code using declarative JSON/YAML templates. Elastic Beanstalk automatically deploys web applications; it does not define arbitrary infrastructure from templates."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"An administrator wants to automate routine tasks, such as starting and stopping Amazon EC2 instances, by writing shell scripts that call AWS services from a terminal. Which tool should they use?","o":["AWS Management Console","AWS CLI","AWS Elastic Beanstalk","Amazon CloudWatch"],"a":[1],"e":"The AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI) manages AWS services from a terminal and is easy to use in scripts. The Management Console is a graphical interface, so it cannot be scripted."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"An operations team needs to patch operating systems, run commands, and connect to hundreds of EC2 instances and on-premises servers without opening SSH ports. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Systems Manager","AWS Config","AWS CloudFormation","Amazon Inspector"],"a":[0],"e":"Systems Manager provides Patch Manager, Run Command, and Session Manager to manage server fleets without opening SSH ports. Config only records and evaluates resource configurations; it does not run commands."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company wants to let employees self-provision only IT-approved products, such as preconfigured CloudFormation templates, while enforcing governance. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Control Tower","AWS Organizations","AWS Service Catalog","AWS License Manager"],"a":[2],"e":"Service Catalog offers a catalog of IT-approved products that users can launch themselves under governance. Control Tower sets up a multi-account landing zone, not a product catalog."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company wants to quickly set up a secure, well-architected multi-account environment (landing zone) with preconfigured guardrails. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Service Catalog","AWS Control Tower","AWS Config","AWS Systems Manager"],"a":[1],"e":"Control Tower automates setting up a multi-account landing zone with guardrails, using Organizations underneath. Service Catalog is a catalog of approved products to deploy."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company wants to centrally manage multiple AWS accounts, use consolidated billing, and apply service control policies (SCPs). Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Organizations","AWS Control Tower","AWS IAM Identity Center","AWS Service Catalog"],"a":[0],"e":"Organizations centrally manages multiple accounts with consolidated billing and SCPs. Control Tower is built on top of Organizations to automate landing zone setup."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company wants to track and enforce usage limits of its Microsoft and Oracle software licenses across AWS and on-premises servers. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Service Catalog","AWS Systems Manager Inventory","AWS Marketplace","AWS License Manager"],"a":[3],"e":"License Manager tracks and enforces software license usage (including BYOL) such as Microsoft and Oracle. AWS Marketplace is where you buy third-party software."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company wants to collect CPU utilization metrics for EC2 instances and trigger an alarm when usage exceeds 80%. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Config","AWS X-Ray"],"a":[1],"e":"CloudWatch collects metrics and logs and creates alarms on thresholds. CloudTrail records who called which API, not performance metrics."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A security team needs to determine which IAM user terminated an EC2 instance yesterday and from which IP address. Which service provides this information?","o":["Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Config","AWS CloudTrail","AWS X-Ray"],"a":[2],"e":"CloudTrail records API activity in the account: who did what, when, and from which IP. Config records resource configuration changes, not who made the API call."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company needs to record configuration changes to its AWS resources over time and evaluate whether resources comply with rules, such as 'all EBS volumes must be encrypted'. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Config","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Trusted Advisor"],"a":[0],"e":"Config records resource configuration history and evaluates compliance with Config rules. CloudTrail records API calls, not resource configuration state and compliance."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"Developers need to trace requests as they travel through a distributed microservices application to identify performance bottlenecks. Which service should they use?","o":["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS Config","AWS X-Ray"],"a":[3],"e":"X-Ray provides distributed tracing to follow requests across services and find bottlenecks. CloudTrail records calls to AWS APIs, not request flow inside your application."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company wants personalized notifications about AWS scheduled maintenance and service events that affect its specific resources. Which should it use?","o":["Amazon CloudWatch dashboards","AWS Health Dashboard","AWS CloudTrail","AWS Trusted Advisor"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Health Dashboard (your account health) gives personalized information on AWS events and maintenance that affect your resources. CloudWatch monitors your resources' metrics, not events on the AWS side."},
  {"d": 3, "t": "management", "q": "Which AWS services help with auditing and governance by tracking API activity and resource configuration history? (Choose TWO.)", "o": ["Amazon CloudFront", "AWS CloudTrail", "AWS X-Ray", "AWS Config", "Amazon Quick"], "a": [1, 3], "e": "CloudTrail tracks API activity and Config tracks resource configuration history, both key for auditing. X-Ray traces application performance."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A company wants to migrate an on-premises Oracle database to Amazon RDS for Oracle while keeping the source database operational during the migration to minimize downtime. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS DataSync","AWS Application Migration Service","AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)","AWS Transfer Family"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS DMS migrates databases with minimal downtime because the source stays operational while changes are continuously replicated. Application Migration Service rehosts whole servers, not databases."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A company is migrating from Oracle to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL and needs to convert the database schema and stored procedures to the new engine. Which tool should it use?","o":["AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT)","AWS DataSync","AWS Application Migration Service","AWS Transfer Family"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS SCT converts database schemas and code (such as stored procedures) for heterogeneous migrations between engines; AWS DMS Schema Conversion offers the same capability as a managed feature of DMS. DataSync only moves files, and Application Migration Service rehosts servers; neither converts schemas."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A company wants to lift and shift (rehost) hundreds of on-premises physical and virtual servers to Amazon EC2 with minimal changes and minimal downtime. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Database Migration Service","AWS Application Migration Service","AWS DataSync","AWS Transfer Family"],"a":[1],"e":"Application Migration Service (MGN) rehosts servers to EC2 using continuous replication for minimal downtime. DMS is only for databases, not whole servers."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A company wants to move files from its on-premises applications into Amazon S3 over the network. The applications must keep using NFS and SMB file shares during and after the move, with recently used files cached locally. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon EBS","AWS Application Migration Service","AWS Storage Gateway","Amazon CloudFront"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS Storage Gateway (S3 File Gateway) presents NFS and SMB file shares on premises, stores the files as objects in Amazon S3, and caches recent data locally. Application Migration Service rehosts whole servers, and EBS is block storage for EC2. (AWS DataSync, often used for one-time bulk copies, is not on the CLF-C02 in-scope service list.)"},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A company needs to move 500 TB from its data center to Amazon S3 and will keep transferring large datasets every month. Its internet connection is far too slow. It can wait a few weeks for setup and wants a dedicated, high-bandwidth private link. Which option meets this requirement?","o":["AWS Client VPN","S3 Transfer Acceleration","Amazon Route 53","AWS Direct Connect"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Direct Connect provides dedicated private connections (up to 100 Gbps) between the data center and AWS that do not use the internet, so large and recurring transfers are fast and consistent. Client VPN and S3 Transfer Acceleration still depend on the slow internet link. (Offline options such as Snowball Edge and AWS Data Transfer Terminal are not on the CLF-C02 in-scope service list.)"},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"Before deciding to migrate, a company's leadership wants a data-driven business case that projects the cost of running its current on-premises servers on AWS, based on their actual utilization. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Budgets","Migration Evaluator","AWS Application Migration Service","AWS Cost Explorer"],"a":[1],"e":"Migration Evaluator collects on-premises utilization data and builds a business case with projected AWS costs for right-sized resources. Cost Explorer and Budgets only analyze or track spending that is already on AWS, and Application Migration Service performs the migration itself."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"Before migrating, a company wants to automatically collect information about its on-premises servers, including utilization data and dependencies between applications. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Application Migration Service","AWS Application Discovery Service","AWS Database Migration Service","AWS Config"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Application Discovery Service collects server inventory, utilization, and network dependency data from on-premises environments to plan migrations, and shares it with AWS Migration Hub. Application Migration Service performs the migration, not discovery. Note: AWS now steers new customers to AWS Transform for discovery, but the exam guide lists Application Discovery Service."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"Which AWS services can help move large amounts of data from an on-premises data center into AWS? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon Inspector","AWS Direct Connect","AWS CloudTrail","AWS Storage Gateway","AWS Well-Architected Tool"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Direct Connect provides a dedicated high-bandwidth network link for large transfers, and Storage Gateway moves on-premises file, volume, and tape data into AWS storage such as S3. Inspector scans for vulnerabilities, CloudTrail logs API calls, and the Well-Architected Tool reviews architectures."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A manufacturer wants to securely connect millions of IoT devices to the cloud using MQTT and route their messages to other AWS services. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS IoT Greengrass","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","AWS IoT Core","Amazon MQ"],"a":[2],"e":"IoT Core securely connects IoT devices to the cloud over MQTT and routes their messages to other AWS services. Greengrass runs compute on local edge devices."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A company wants contractors to use internal web applications and SaaS tools from their own unmanaged laptops through a secure browser, with no company data stored on the device and no full virtual desktop. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon WorkSpaces","Amazon WorkSpaces Secure Browser","Amazon AppStream 2.0","AWS Client VPN"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon WorkSpaces Secure Browser gives users secure, browser-based access to internal websites and SaaS apps without storing data on the device. WorkSpaces provides full virtual desktops, AppStream 2.0 streams desktop applications, and Client VPN would put unmanaged devices on the corporate network."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A company wants to provide employees with persistent, fully managed Windows or Linux virtual desktops that they can access from anywhere. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon AppStream 2.0","Amazon Lightsail","Amazon EC2 Dedicated Hosts","Amazon WorkSpaces"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon WorkSpaces (WorkSpaces Personal) provides persistent, fully managed virtual desktops (DaaS) for employees. Amazon AppStream 2.0 streams specific applications to a browser, not a full persistent desktop."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A software company wants to stream a specific desktop application to users' web browsers without users installing it or needing a full virtual desktop. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon WorkSpaces","Amazon AppStream 2.0","AWS Amplify Hosting","Amazon CloudFront"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon AppStream 2.0 streams desktop applications to a web browser with no installation. (In the AWS console it has been renamed Amazon WorkSpaces Applications.) Amazon WorkSpaces provides an entire virtual desktop, not just one application."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A company wants to set up an omnichannel cloud contact center with voice and chat for its customer service agents. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Lex","Amazon Connect","Amazon SNS","Amazon Chime SDK"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon Connect is an omnichannel cloud contact center service. Lex can be used inside Connect as a chatbot, but it is not a contact center itself."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"An application needs to send transactional emails such as order confirmations and password resets at scale. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon SNS","Amazon SQS","Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)","Amazon Connect"],"a":[2],"e":"SES is an email service for sending transactional and marketing email at scale. SNS can send simple email notifications to topic subscribers, but it is not an email service for application customers."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A company wants each smart thermostat to authenticate with its own X.509 certificate and wants the cloud to keep a copy of each device's latest reported state (a device shadow) even while the device is offline. Which service provides this?","o":["Amazon Connect","AWS Outposts","AWS IoT Core","Amazon SES"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS IoT Core securely connects devices using per-device certificates and keeps device shadows that store each device's last reported and desired state. Outposts brings AWS infrastructure on premises, and Connect and SES are contact center and email services."},
  {"d":3,"t":"global-infra","q":"A company must keep certain workloads in its own on-premises data center for data residency and low latency, but wants to use the same AWS infrastructure, APIs, and tools there. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Local Zones","AWS Wavelength","AWS Outposts","AWS Direct Connect"],"a":[2],"e":"Outposts puts AWS racks and servers in your own data center with the same AWS APIs and tools. Local Zones are AWS-owned infrastructure placed near large cities, not at your location."},
  {"d":3,"t":"global-infra","q":"A media company needs single-digit millisecond latency for video rendering workloads for users in a large metropolitan area that is far from the nearest AWS Region. Which option should it use?","o":["AWS Outposts","AWS Local Zones","AWS Wavelength","Amazon CloudFront"],"a":[1],"e":"Local Zones place AWS compute and storage near large metropolitan areas for single-digit millisecond latency. Wavelength runs only inside telecom providers' 5G networks."},
  {"d":3,"t":"global-infra","q":"A company is building an application for mobile devices that requires ultra-low latency by running compute at the edge of telecommunications providers' 5G networks. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Local Zones","AWS Outposts","AWS Global Accelerator","AWS Wavelength"],"a":[3],"e":"Wavelength embeds AWS compute inside telecom providers' 5G networks for ultra-low latency to mobile devices. Local Zones are near cities but not inside 5G networks."},
  {"d":3,"t":"global-infra","q":"A company wants its application to remain available if a single data center fails, while keeping all resources within one AWS Region. What should it do?","o":["Deploy across multiple Availability Zones","Deploy to multiple edge locations","Use a single larger EC2 instance","Use AWS Local Zones only"],"a":[0],"e":"An Availability Zone is one or more separate data centers within a Region, so deploying across multiple AZs survives a data center failure. Edge locations are for caching and DNS, not for running the main application."},
  {"d":3,"t":"global-infra","q":"Which AWS services use edge locations to serve users with lower latency? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon CloudFront","Amazon EBS","Amazon Route 53","Amazon RDS","AWS Outposts"],"a":[0,2],"e":"CloudFront (CDN) and Route 53 (DNS) run at edge locations worldwide. EBS and RDS run inside Availability Zones within a Region."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"Which statements describe Amazon EC2 instance store? (Choose TWO.)","o":["It can be detached and attached to a different instance","Data is lost when the instance is stopped or terminated","It is automatically backed up to Amazon S3","It is physically attached to the host computer","It can be shared with other instances over NFS"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Instance store is disk physically attached to the host, and its data is lost when the instance stops or terminates. EBS volumes can be detached and reattached, and EFS can be shared over NFS."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"Which statements about security groups are correct? (Choose TWO.)","o":["They are stateful","They operate at the subnet level","They support explicit deny rules","They act as a firewall at the instance (network interface) level","They are stateless"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Security groups are stateful and act as a firewall at the instance (ENI) level with allow rules only. Subnet level, stateless, and deny rules are characteristics of network ACLs."},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"Which statements describe Amazon SQS? (Choose TWO.)","o":["It pushes messages directly to email and SMS subscribers","Consumers poll the queue to retrieve messages","Standard queues guarantee strict message ordering","Messages are stored until they are processed and deleted, or the retention period expires","It runs SQL queries on streaming data"],"a":[1,3],"e":"SQS consumers poll the queue, and messages are kept until deleted or the retention period expires. Pushing to email/SMS is SNS, and strict ordering is only guaranteed by FIFO queues."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"Which are benefits of using Amazon RDS instead of running a database on Amazon EC2? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Full root access to the database host operating system","Automated backups","No need to choose an instance size","Automated patching of the database engine","Unlimited free storage"],"a":[1,3],"e":"RDS is a managed service: AWS handles automated backups and database engine patching. Full root access to the host OS is only available when you run the database yourself on EC2."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"A company receives scanned invoices and wants to extract the text and then identify entities and key phrases in that text. Which services should it use? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon Textract","Amazon Lex","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Polly"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Textract extracts text from scanned documents, then Comprehend analyzes that text for entities and key phrases. Transcribe is for audio, not documents."},
  {"d": 3, "t": "analytics", "q": "A company wants a serverless way to catalog datasets stored in Amazon S3 and then query them using standard SQL. Which services should it use? (Choose TWO.)", "o": ["Amazon EMR", "AWS Glue", "Amazon MSK", "Amazon Athena", "Amazon Quick"], "a": [1, 3], "e": "Glue crawlers and the Data Catalog catalog the S3 data, then Athena queries it with standard SQL, all serverless. EMR requires a cluster, and Amazon Quick (formerly QuickSight) is for visualization."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"Within a CI/CD pipeline on AWS, which services orchestrate the release workflow and build and test the code? (Choose TWO.)","o":["AWS CloudTrail","AWS CodeBuild","AWS Config","AWS CodePipeline","Amazon Inspector"],"a":[1,3],"e":"CodePipeline orchestrates the release stages (source, build, test, deploy), and CodeBuild compiles code and runs tests. CloudTrail and Config are audit and governance services, not CI/CD stages."}
];
